WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.534
-Our next guest is a
talented actor who you know from

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:05.200
films such as "Mudbound,"
"Chappaquiddick,"

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.501
and "Zero Dark Thirty."

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:08.000
You can catch him in
the highly anticipated series

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.701
"Winning Time:
The Rise of the Lakers Dynasty,"

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:13.634
premiering March 6th on
HBO and streaming on HBO Max.

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.167
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:16.701
-You know what?

00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:19.501
Cooke sent me out there
to be his star, too.

00:00:19.501 --> 00:00:21.334
Got me to sell his tickets,

00:00:21.334 --> 00:00:25.167
and every year, when we lost,
he still cashed his checks,

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.133
but I'm the one
that's got to live with it.

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.400
I'm the one that's got to have
the nightmares

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.334
with these
Boston Irish mother[bleep]

00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:32.534
running the floor.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:35.334
-The always-hustling,
always-chasing Jerry West

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:36.667
with a final tally
that was heartbreaking.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.267
-Do you think I like that?

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:41.033
-A series that could have
truly gone either way.

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:43.334
-I can still smell
the halitosis.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:46.300
Do you have any idea
what that's like?

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.567
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:49.300
Jason Clarke, everybody!

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.367
Welcome back, Jason.

00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:11.167
-I tell you what,
I'm keeping up

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:13.400
the sartorial standard here
on the show, aren't I?

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.167
-I mean, you're setting a way
higher bar than the rest of us.

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.434
-No, I saw Oliver out there.

00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:19.400
I should have come
'90s Australian plumber,

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.467
and I did
'70s Italian industrialist.

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:27.767
-Yeah, there's, like, a
shipping-magnate vibe to you.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.234
It's really nice.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:30.968
-Cashmere socks, Seth.
-Really?

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:32.400
-Yeah.
-Cashmere socks?

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.968
You didn't get the memo that
we're slummin' it these days.

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:35.334
-No, I know.

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:38.200
I should have put the
Blundstones on and the RMs, eh?

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.934
-You -- I want to ask, because,
one of the most fun parts,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.300
I would imagine, about playing
this era Jerry West

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.667
on the show is the sort of
sartorial choices

00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:46.734
he made in the '80s.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:48.767
It's a fun --
The look of this show

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.934
is sort of all
the joyful colors of that.

00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:53.701
This is --
That's real Jerry West.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:55.100
They really --
You did a good job there.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.434
-No, they did.

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:59.501
They bought -- Because he was
into Missoni sweaters,

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:02.434
and they bought these beautiful
Missoni knits off of eBay,

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:04.834
you know, resellers that were
original from the period.

00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:06.701
It was -- I mean,
everyone had their style.

00:02:06.701 --> 00:02:08.634
Big, white feather coats
going on.

00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:11.534
I mean, it's
Magic mother[bleep] Johnson.

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:12.767
-Yeah.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:14.400
And it's a fantastic cast.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.133
Did you know much --
I mean, obviously,

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:17.200
you grew up in Australia.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.834
Did you know much about
the '80s Lakers?

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:21.033
Do you ever go to
the Lakers games now?

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:22.868
-Yeah, I went last night
with John Reilly.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:23.868
-Alright, look at that.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:25.501
-John C. Reilly,
who's fantastic in this.

00:02:25.501 --> 00:02:27.667
-Yeah. Gets a lot of love, John.
He really does.

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:30.033
-Yeah, he's -- Yeah.
-He gets a lot of love.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:31.467
It was funny,
because it was a bad --

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.667
The lakers played terribly.

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:36.567
I mean, terribly.
Mike Tyson left at halftime.

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:38.501
-Yeah.

00:02:38.501 --> 00:02:40.100
-And it was --
Because I was there with John

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.334
and, you know, young Quincy,
who's playing Magic,

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.801
and DeVaughn,
who's Norm Nixon's son.

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:47.200
And they got their butts kicked.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.200
-Yeah. That's not good.
You guys are a jinx.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.067
-But, you know,
the biggest thing I knew was

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:57.067
when Magic was
diagnosed HIV-positive,

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:58.467
which is where our show
kind of ends,

00:02:58.467 --> 00:03:00.100
in the flashback
at the beginning.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.501
And then he played in
Barcelona at the Olympics.

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:02.834
-Yeah.

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:05.501
-And in Australia, we had these
ads with the Grim Reaper

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.467
for AIDS bowling the ball down,
and, you know,

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:09.934
it was very doom and gloom,
and maybe rightly so.

00:03:09.934 --> 00:03:14.334
But there he was, you know,
6'10" of him

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:16.501
playing, you know,
with the Dream Team,

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:17.934
and everyone
was playing with him, you know?

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:19.501
And he was living with it.

00:03:19.501 --> 00:03:20.767
And it was like, "Oh, wow.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.868
There's another
world out there, as well,"

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.834
which is -- You know,
it was one of those things that,

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:26.767
even in Australia,
little, old country boy

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:28.033
from Australia went,
"Look at this dude, man.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:29.334
Look at him."

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:32.701
-And you were really a
country boy from Australia.

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:34.567
Your dad was a sheep shearer.
Is that --

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:36.200
-Yes, he was. Yeah.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.734
-That sounds like the punch line
of a joke about somebody.

00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:44.567
Did he think that acting was
a noble profession?

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:46.534
Like, how does somebody --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:50.167
-I was the butt
of a lot of jokes in my

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:52.300
wider-ranging family
for a long time.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.367
-Yeah. Like, how did you
even approach to him

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.267
that this was going to be a path
you wanted your life to take?

00:03:58.267 --> 00:03:59.601
-You know,
it was kind of like --

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:02.467
I mean, my dad is
the loveliest, gentlest man,

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:05.400
who had a very rough upbringing.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.534
He just didn't know
that it was a path.

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:07.634
-Yeah.

00:04:07.634 --> 00:04:09.033
-He didn't know that it
was a profession.

00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:11.801
You know, it was like -- I was
doing an interview with --

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:13.133
The shows are very different.

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:16.300
I mean, you know, Australian
interview the other day

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.701
was saying,
"Oh, you're playing Jerry West."

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:18.968
You know,
and all the other guys,

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:20.100
the Americans on the panel,
are going,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.234
"Wow, you know, Jerry --"

00:04:21.234 --> 00:04:23.267
And, "You look like
Frankie J. Holden."

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:25.167
And nobody would know who
Frankie J. Holden is.

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:27.634
He's this Australian actor
from the '70s.

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:31.100
But it's always taking it back
to that small time,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.067
"How does this relate
to Australia?"

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:36.968
So, my father had no idea,
you know, that being an actor

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:39.567
was a profession
and it's something you could do.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:42.634
-Like, once you took that path
and once you sort of met

00:04:42.634 --> 00:04:44.434
with some success,
was he happy for you?

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:45.701
Was he excited?
-Yeah.

00:04:45.701 --> 00:04:46.801
He's a big golfer,
and I've gotten him onto some

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:47.801
golf courses, yeah.

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:48.934
-Oh, there you go.

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:51.634
So it's a real quid pro quo
situation, yeah.

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:52.701
-No, it was,
because Jerry West was

00:04:52.701 --> 00:04:54.167
a big golfer, as well.

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:59.400
He actually held the back-nine
record, I think, at Bel-Air.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.534
And the pictures
that my father loved the best,

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:02.567
when I'd send him, you know,

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:04.968
was in the '80s golf gear,
you know?

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:07.100
I mean, the colors
were just magnificent.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.167
The tight, checked pants.

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:10.868
You know, the white shoes
and the socks,

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:13.901
I mean, and, you know,
the big, gold watches,

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:16.834
and the ring, of course,
his one ring.

00:05:16.834 --> 00:05:20.133
-And you also -- I should note,
there are some flashbacks,

00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:22.601
and you got to play Jerry West
the basketball player,

00:05:22.601 --> 00:05:23.601
not the executive.

00:05:23.601 --> 00:05:24.601
-In the tight shorts.

00:05:24.601 --> 00:05:25.834
-Yeah, well,
that's my question.

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:28.133
-Yeah. I worked my buns for
a long time, dude.

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:30.234
-Yeah. Well, it shows up.

00:05:30.234 --> 00:05:31.701
I just want to tip my cap.

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:33.667
You know, the work you put in
pays off.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:35.901
-A bit funny, though, because I
have very bandy legs.

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:37.801
My legs are very bowed.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:40.267
-And it's because my father said
he grew up on a horse,

00:05:40.267 --> 00:05:42.734
so I must have come out
the same way.

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:44.334
Because before he was a shearer,

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:46.467
he was a drover as a young boy.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:50.167
He had to drive cattle across
Australia at like 7 and 8.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:51.367
-They make you
drive cattle at --

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:54.367
-Well, he did.
Yeah, it's some tough love.

00:05:54.367 --> 00:05:56.734
He got an orange
for Christmas, dude.

00:05:56.734 --> 00:05:58.734
Between eight of them.
I mean, and that's a fact.

00:05:58.734 --> 00:05:59.868
He did.

00:05:59.868 --> 00:06:02.133
He goes, "We got an orange
for Christmas.

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:03.400
There was eight of us."

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.634
-I can't believe, by the way --

00:06:04.634 --> 00:06:06.901
Like, you can never complain
about work to him.

00:06:06.901 --> 00:06:08.567
You can't be like, "Oh, my God,
we had a 14-hour day on set."

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:11.334
-No.
-He's like, "Got that orange."

00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:15.901
-But I did send him a picture
of me in the gear, you know,

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:17.567
the knitted top and the shorts.

00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:19.234
And all him and mum pointed out

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:20.834
and laughed at
was my bandy legs.

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.400
"Oh, we showed everyone, Jason."

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.901
"Well, I said not to show
anybody, because --"

00:06:24.901 --> 00:06:27.033
They looked at those legs,
you know?

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:28.901
Yep. And I thought, "Well,
what about my upper torso?

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:30.100
Didn't I look muscly?"

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.567
-No. That's --
Parents will often find --

00:06:32.567 --> 00:06:34.534
Yeah, well,
maybe he's just proud that you

00:06:34.534 --> 00:06:36.667
have his bandy
legs, and he just went --

00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:37.701
-You know what, Seth?
I think he is.

00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:38.734
I think he is.

00:06:38.734 --> 00:06:40.767
I do hope my sons
don't have them, though.

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:44.033
-Yeah. Is it too early to tell
if your sons have your legs?

00:06:44.033 --> 00:06:46.367
Because I know one of my kids
has my legs, 100%.

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:48.000
-Does he?
-Yeah.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.000
-My youngest has got my hands,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.567
my short, stubbly,
working-man hands.

00:06:53.567 --> 00:06:57.701
My oldest has got my wife's
longer, piano-playing fingers.

00:06:57.701 --> 00:06:58.901
-That's so funny.

00:06:58.901 --> 00:07:00.801
-But I think we've done
a personality swap.

00:07:00.801 --> 00:07:01.901
You know what I mean?

00:07:01.901 --> 00:07:04.534
One looks like me,
but he's his mother,

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:06.067
and one looks like my wife,
but he's me.

00:07:06.067 --> 00:07:07.300
-That's great.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.467
I feel like that's the most fun
possible outcome.

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:12.334
Yeah.
We're doing the same thing.

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:13.901
We were talking
a little bit about it.

00:07:13.901 --> 00:07:16.234
I've got a 5-year-old
up on skis.

00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:18.267
Your 5-year-old --
also up on skis.

00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:20.367
Is he a good skier?

00:07:20.367 --> 00:07:24.801
-You know, he did his second
time in France.

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:26.601
First, we went to Mammoth.

00:07:26.601 --> 00:07:28.367
And he came off the mountain
after going up

00:07:28.367 --> 00:07:30.701
on the lift, you know,
and my wife timed it there

00:07:30.701 --> 00:07:32.467
so I'd catch him, you know?

00:07:32.467 --> 00:07:36.200
He'd grown like 2 feet higher
in pride, you know?

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.901
"Papa! Papa!"

00:07:37.901 --> 00:07:40.334
Smack! Straight into the thing.

00:07:40.334 --> 00:07:41.968
And it's like, "Oh, no!"

00:07:41.968 --> 00:07:44.834
"It's okay! I'm okay, Papa."
I'm okay!"

00:07:44.834 --> 00:07:48.200
My wife's French,
so he calls me "Papa,"

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.267
which I love, rather than "Dad."

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:51.200
-Oh, that's really good.
-Yeah.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.200
But you saddled up
for a third, dude.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.033
-Yeah, we got a third.
-I feel inadequate.

00:07:55.033 --> 00:07:56.801
-I was like --
I wanted to get a third.

00:07:56.801 --> 00:07:59.400
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you. Yeah.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.767
And I'm realizing now,

00:08:00.767 --> 00:08:02.901
it's not too late to make her
call me "Papa."

00:08:02.901 --> 00:08:04.434
The other two, it's lost.

00:08:04.434 --> 00:08:06.467
It'd be great to have two
that call me "Dad"

00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:08.067
and then a
little girl who's like, "Papa!"

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:09.534
-Papa, Papa!

00:08:09.534 --> 00:08:10.767
-And then
I could always be like,

00:08:10.767 --> 00:08:14.267
"That's why she gets my
attention, you two dopes.

00:08:14.267 --> 00:08:15.534
Because she's charming."

00:08:15.534 --> 00:08:17.801
-It's true, though, isn't it?
-Yeah.

00:08:17.801 --> 00:08:21.100
-Yeah, there's something about
when your little kid

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.534
comes off a mountain
and he's taller,

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:24.400
and his pride
and he looks at you

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.434
and he just wants to let you --
"Did you see that?"

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:27.801
-It's really great.

00:08:27.801 --> 00:08:31.434
Yeah, we -- Although,
I went down with him on Sunday,

00:08:31.434 --> 00:08:35.801
because he can barrel down,
and we're working on turns.

00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:37.133
And so we went down.
-Important.

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:38.501
-Yeah, yeah.
-"Dude, come back!

00:08:38.501 --> 00:08:39.834
I can't come after you."

00:08:39.834 --> 00:08:41.534
-And he was doing his turns,
and it was really good.

00:08:41.534 --> 00:08:44.234
And I was filming him as he was
doing his turn, on the iPhone.

00:08:44.234 --> 00:08:46.033
And then I realized,
"I'm going to break my neck."

00:08:46.033 --> 00:08:47.834
Like, that's --
And he'll always be like,

00:08:47.834 --> 00:08:49.234
"This is the first time
I did turns,

00:08:49.234 --> 00:08:50.534
and that's when Daddy went off
the side of the mountain."

00:08:50.534 --> 00:08:53.000
-"And that's Daddy going --"
Yeah.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.567
And, there, he's going.
And he's still going.

00:08:55.567 --> 00:08:58.000
He's still -- Yep.
Yep, he's still falling.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.133
-He's still falling.

00:08:59.133 --> 00:09:00.834
-Somebody will find
the phone later on.

00:09:00.834 --> 00:09:02.968
-They're like, "Oh, God,
this video started so nice,

00:09:02.968 --> 00:09:04.934
and then -- Yeah."

00:09:04.934 --> 00:09:06.234
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:06.234 --> 00:09:08.300
It's always such a pleasure.
Congrats on the show.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.467
-Thank you.
-Jason Clarke.

00:09:09.467 --> 00:09:11.701
"Winning Time"
premieres March 6th

00:09:11.701 --> 00:09:13.133
on HBO and streaming on HBO Max.

